=== Complex Drills

Each of the following drills is made up of a series of steps and are designed to be practised progressively—first step one, once that works at full speed, step one and two, then one and two and three, etc.
Start each iteration slower than you think and speed it up to something like sparring speed over 4--8 repetitions.

The goal of each action is to hit the opponent while not getting hit, but it is not the goal of the drill to win at all costs.
Each action should counter the previous action, but especially as speed and intensity increase, not every action will be successful.
That is fine.
Analyse what went wrong and try again.
As with the simple drills, focus on balance and on using it to initiate each action.

These drills encourages a style of fencing that emphasises control over explosiveness.
Control does not mean strength, however.
A weaker fencer with good structure and good technique (strong on weak, etc.) has little issue controlling a much stronger fencer.
Nonetheless, fencers who have based their style on being faster than their opponents may find it frustrating.

==== First Drill
Fencers (A and B) start in middle distance (where either can hit the other with a lunge).
+ A binds onto B’s blade on the inside or outside; B disengages; A rebinds. On a disengage of their
choice, A lunges, striking B with a thrust in opposition instead of rebinding.
+ If A attacks on the inside, B voids with a volte or scanzo; if A attacks on the outside, B voids
with a traverso L. In either case, B ‘leaves’ their blade in the middle line, as described in section
1 above.
+ A parries B’s blade with their empty hand, and strikes B with a stramazone to the head. The
stramazone can be done with or without a step of the back foot.

==== Second Drill
Fencers start at a distance where either can strike the other with a gathering step and a lunge.
+ A binds onto B’s blade on the inside or outside with a gathering step; B disengages and lunges, striking A with a thrust in opposition.
+ If B attacks on the inside, A voids with a volte or scanzo; if B attacks on the outside, A voids with a traverso L. In either case, A ‘leaves’ their blade in the middle line, as described in section 1 above.
+ As 2 above, but instead of disengaging and striking, B may instead take a step backwards. If they do, A finishes the gathering step as a step forward, effectively resetting the situation.
+ As 3 above, but from the initial position, either fencer may initiate the action with a gatheringstep. At this point, this drill begins to resemble a constrained for of sparring, and the intensity should eventually reflect that.

==== Third Drill
This drill combines elements of the two drills above. Fencers start at a distance where either can strike the other with a gathering step and a lunge.
+ A binds onto B’s blade on the inside or outside with a gathering step; B disengages and lunges, striking A with a thrust in opposition.
+ A either takes B’s disengage as a tempo to strike them with a thrust in opposition,1 or voids B’s attack with a volte/scanzo or traverso L, depending on whether B attacks on the inside or the outside.
+ If A attacks into B’s disengage, B voids A’s attack with a volte/scanzo or traverso L.
+ If B voids, A parries B’s blade with their empty hand, and strikes B with a stramazone to the head. The stramazone can be done with or without a step of the back foot. If A voids, B parries with the empty hand, etc.
+ Instead of disengaging and striking when a seeks the bind, B may take a step backwards. If they do, A finishes the gathering step as a step forward, effectively resetting the situation.
+ As 5 above, but from the initial position, either fencer may initiate the action by seeking the bind with a gathering step. Adding this step makes for an extremely complex drill and in most cases it is best left out.

==== Improvising Drills
Once two fencers have repertoire of actions and are comfortable moving within the idiom of their style (whether that is as broad as ‘seventeenth-century Italian rapier’ or as narrow as ‘Rapier after #cite(<capoferroRidolfoCapoFerro>, form: "author")'), they can begin to improvise drills like the ones above. Doing so is a great awareness drill, and can be tailored to reinforce a fencer’s strength or to address their weaknesses. Drills like these can also given a focus other than balance, e.g. moving off line, using the empty hand, blade- foot coordination, etc. I have found the following guidelines to be helpful:
- Agree on a starting distance and action, e.g. a beat and a thrust, seeking a bind, etc. The drills above are ambidextrous and symmetrical, but that is not a requirement.
- Each iteration of the drill should be done slowly and then sped up over 4–8 repetitions.
- Regardless of the speed, every action should be done with full force aiming at good targets.
- Try to keep each ‘turn’ to one or, at most, two actions each.
- Each action after the first should be what Rory Miller calls a golden move @miller2016TrainingSuddenViolence: it protects you, hurts your opponent, puts you in a better position, puts your opponent in a worse position.
- Keep the exchanges relatively short (2–5 ‘turns’ at most), otherwise what started as a fencing drill becomes a memory game.
